{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Complete A2 by removing the steady-state legacy field, so successful new-writer passes leave `bindings.json` with no rotation bookkeeping. This is the irreversible half of A2. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** contains the measured facts; do not re-derive them.\n\n## Compatibility and evidence gate (no fixed delay)\n\n- A2-1 is merged to `main`.\n- A completed `reconcile-bridge.yml` run has a `github.sha` that is a descendant of the A2-1 merge commit (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`). The workflow installs from that checked-out tree, so this directly proves the ephemeral writer executed the sidecar-capable build.\n- Before closure, explicitly refresh the host probe binary from a descendant of the A2-1 merge, record its executable path and version/source, and prove no older reconcile/probe process remains. Do not substitute elapsed time for these observations.\n\nThe writer set is the GHA reconciler and local probe runs. The canary runs only `bridge-fsck` plus ticket CLI operations; the AWS review bot never writes `bindings.json`. Mixed-version safety remains in code: a legacy writer may temporarily reintroduce an inline stamp, but the next new writer reads `max(sidecar, legacy)`, stages that maximum in both in-memory forms, persists the sidecar first, and scrubs the inline field only after sidecar persistence succeeds. An overlooked old process therefore causes bounded churn and self-heals instead of losing rotation progress.\n\nThe supported rollback floor after A2-2 is the A2-1 merge: any A2-1-or-newer binary reads both forms. A rollback below A2-1 is deliberately unsupported after the deployment evidence gate. Such a pre-sidecar binary would see scrubbed inline state as empty and forfeit only the rotation optimization, causing bounded extra GETs and rebuilding coverage within `ceil(N/K)` passes; it does not lose bindings or reconciliation correctness.\n\n## Persistence ordering and failure contract\n\nA successful save must establish this order:\n\n1. Merge every staged inline value into the in-memory sidecar using chronological `max`.\n2. Atomically persist `get_rotation.json`.\n3. Only when sidecar persistence returns `True`, remove inline `last_get_pass` values and atomically write `bindings.json`.\n\n`set_last_get` continues staging the inline value in memory as a failure floor; the successful save path removes it before `bindings.json` is serialized, so steady-state committed output remains sidecar-only. If the fail-open sidecar write returns `False`, the same save retains and writes the inline values to `bindings.json`. If the process fails after the sidecar replace but before the bindings replace, the sidecar already contains the maximum and the old bindings file still contains its legacy floor. Thus every observable one-file or two-file state retains the maximum, and the next successful new-writer pass converges to sidecar-only form.\n\n## Approach\n\nChange `BindingStore.save()` to persist rotation state before serializing the inline-scrubbed binding payload. Keep the mixed-version `max(new, legacy)` read seam. On sidecar failure, persist the binding payload with inline fallback values intact; on the next successful pass, promote their maximum and scrub them. Do not make ordinary reconciliation fail because rotation is an optimization, but do not discard the only durable copy of its maximum.\n\n## Verification plan\n\n- Normal success: a real temporary store advances rotation, saves, reloads with inline fields absent, and preserves exact rotation order from `get_rotation.json`.\n- Fail-open sidecar write: inject a sidecar tempfile/replace failure, prove the primary binding save succeeds with the latest inline floor retained, reload with identical order, then remove the fault and prove the next save promotes and scrubs it.\n- Mid-save crash boundary: let the sidecar replace succeed, inject failure at the subsequent bindings replace, and prove a fresh reload returns the same maximum and rotation order before a later successful convergence.\n- Mixed writer: a legacy writer reintroduces a later inline stamp; the new writer persists that maximum before scrubbing and matches an all-new sequence.\n- Anti-starvation and repository packed-size measurements remain the end-to-end behavior and cost oracles.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] After one successful new-writer save, `last_get_pass` is absent from every entry in `bindings.json`, the sidecar contains the chronological maximum, and rotation order is preserved after reload. Tier: integration in a real temporary git repository.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] When sidecar persistence fails open, the primary binding save succeeds with the latest inline fallback retained; reload preserves the same rotation order, and the next successful save promotes the maximum to `get_rotation.json` before removing the inline field. Tier: integration with injected tempfile and replace failures.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] A failure after the sidecar replace but before the bindings replace cannot lose the maximum: a fresh reload observes the same maximum and a later successful save converges to sidecar-only form. Tier: integration at the two-file persistence boundary.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] Anti-starvation still holds: every bound-but-absent key is serviced within `ceil(N/K)` passes with only the new file present. Tier: unit on the rotation selector.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] An old-writer/new-writer sequence that reintroduces a later inline stamp migrates that maximum into `get_rotation.json`, removes the inline field, and preserves the same subsequent rotation order as an all-new sequence. Tier: integration.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] Gate evidence is recorded on this ticket: A2-1 merge SHA, descendant `reconcile-bridge.yml` run id and successful ancestry result, host probe executable/version after an explicit refresh, and confirmation that no older reconcile/probe process remains.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] Marginal packed cost per changed version is at or below the A2-1 measurement. Tier: repository-level measurement.\n\n## Files\n\n`binding_store.py`, existing rotation helpers as needed, and focused unit/integration tests.\n\nScope: S. Depends on A3 (sequencing A1 -> A2-1 -> A3 -> A2-2)."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785801284198819001,"uuid":"0b17472d-21b9-41da-bd1a-e5a7de7ff273"}